.TH S3AR 1 "August 2026" "S3 Archiver" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
s3ar \- create tar archives from S3 and extract them back to S3
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B s3ar
.RB ( \-c | \-\-create )
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.RB [ \-\-zstd ]
.RB [ \-f " TARFILE" ]
.I S3...
.br
.B s3ar
.RB ( \-x | \-\-extract | \-\-get )
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.RB [ \-\-zstd ]
.RB [ \-f " TARFILE" ]
.RI [ S3... ]
.br
.B s3ar \-\-list-buckets
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.RI [ S3... ]
.br
.B s3ar \-\-list-objects
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.I S3...
.br
.B s3ar
.RB ( \-h | \-\-help )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B s3ar
(S3 Archiver) creates tar archives directly from S3-compatible storage and
restores them back to S3. It preserves object metadata and records bucket ACLs
in PAX
.B SCHILY
extended-attribute headers.
.PP
The command line follows the familiar style of
.BR tar (1)
and supports
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-f ,
and
.BR \-v .
.B s3ar
works with live S3 resources and implements only the options documented here;
it is not a general replacement for
.BR tar .
.SH S3 CONFIGURATION
Set the S3 credentials and endpoint. These values are an example:
.PP
.nf
export S3AR_ACCESS_KEY='access-key'
export S3AR_SECRET_KEY='secret-key'
export S3AR_ENDPOINT='https://s3.example.net'
export S3AR_URI_STYLE='path'
export S3AR_REGION='us-east-1'
export S3AR_SESSION_TOKEN='temporary-session-token'
.fi
.PP
.BR S3AR_ACCESS_KEY ,
.BR S3AR_SECRET_KEY ,
and
.B S3AR_ENDPOINT
are required.
.B S3AR_ENDPOINT
may contain a host and port, but not a URL path. Its scheme may be
.B http://
or
.BR https://
and is required. A trailing slash is not accepted.
.PP
.B S3AR_URI_STYLE
accepts
.B path
or
.B virtual
and defaults to
.BR path .
.B S3AR_REGION
selects the SigV4 signing region and the location constraint used when creating
a bucket that does not already exist. It defaults to
.B us-east-1
when it is not set; for that value,
.B s3ar
omits the location constraint.
.PP
.B S3AR_SESSION_TOKEN
optionally supplies a token for temporary credentials.
.SS Environment variables
.TP
.B S3AR_ACCESS_KEY
S3 access key. Required.
.TP
.B S3AR_SECRET_KEY
S3 secret key. Required.
.TP
.B S3AR_ENDPOINT
S3 endpoint in the form
.BR [http://|https://]HOST[:PORT] .
Required.
.TP
.B S3AR_URI_STYLE
S3 addressing style. Optional; defaults to
.BR path .
.TP
.B S3AR_REGION
SigV4 signing region and location constraint used when creating a bucket that
does not already exist. Optional; defaults to
.BR us-east-1 ,
which omits the location constraint.
.TP
.B S3AR_SESSION_TOKEN
Token for temporary S3 credentials. Optional.
.SH COMMAND LINE
.SS Operations
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-create
Create a tar archive from the selected S3 resources. At least one S3 URI is
required. URIs are processed in command-line order. If overlapping URIs select
an object more than once, it is stored once for each match. Bucket directory
entries are stored only once.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-extract ", " \-\-get
Restore a tar archive to S3. With no S3 URI, the whole archive is restored.
One or more URIs limit extraction to matching entries; overlapping matches do
not upload an entry more than once.
.TP
.B \-\-list-buckets
List all bucket names without listing their objects. Positional arguments are
ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-list-objects
List objects in the selected live S3 resources. At least one S3 URI is
required.
.SS Options
.TP
.BR \-f " " TARFILE ", " \-\-file= TARFILE
Read or write the named archive. This option is valid only with
.B \-\-create
or
.BR \-\-extract .
TARFILE must be a local filesystem path or
.BR \- .
.B \-\-create
truncates an existing file and writes directly to it. If the command fails
after opening the file, a partial archive may remain.
.PP
A new archive starts with mode 0666, modified by the current
.BR umask .
Overwriting an existing archive keeps its permissions.
.TP
.B \-\-zstd
With
.BR \-\-create ,
write a zstd-compressed archive.
.B \-\-extract
detects plain and zstd-compressed input automatically; specifying this option
rejects plain input. The option is not valid with
.B \-\-list-buckets
or
.BR \-\-list-objects .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Enable verbose output. During creation and extraction, write each successfully
processed entry as
.B BUCKET/KEY
to standard error, without the
.B s3://
prefix. With listing operations, include the additional fields described in
.BR "LISTING BUCKETS AND OBJECTS" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display command-line help and exit successfully.
.PP
Without
.BR \-f ,
or with
.BR "\-f \-" ,
.B \-\-create
writes to standard output and
.B \-\-extract
reads from standard input.
.SH NAME QUOTING
Names in listings, verbose output, and diagnostics use byte-oriented URL
encoding. ASCII letters, digits,
.BR - ,
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
.BR ~ ,
and
.B /
are written unchanged. Every other byte is written as
.B %HH
with uppercase hexadecimal digits. A space is therefore
.BR %20 ,
a literal percent sign is
.BR %25 ,
and UTF-8 names are encoded byte by byte. The format is independent of the
current locale.
.SH SELECTING S3 RESOURCES
A trailing slash does not change the selection.
.TP
.B s3://
Select all buckets and all their objects.
.TP
.BR s3://BUCKET " or " s3://BUCKET/
Select the named bucket and all its objects.
.TP
.BR s3://BUCKET/NAME " or " s3://BUCKET/NAME/
Select the exact
.B NAME
key and all objects below
.BR NAME/ .
.PP
Matches stop at path boundaries. For example,
.B photo
matches the exact key
.B photo
and keys below
.BR photo/ ,
but not
.B photo1.jpg
or
.BR photo-old.jpg .
The exact key itself does not need to exist if matching descendants do.
.PP
If a URI matches neither an object nor a prefix, the command fails. Empty
buckets are valid and still produce a bucket entry during archive creation.
Input URIs use the
.B s3://
form even though listing and verbose output omit that prefix.
.PP
The examples below assume that S3 contains these objects:
.PP
.nf
s3://photos/2026/photo1.jpg
s3://photos/2026/photo2.jpg
s3://photos/2026/photoN.jpg
s3://photos/2027/photo1.jpg
s3://photos/2027/photo2.jpg
s3://photos/2027/photoN.jpg
s3://videos/2026/video1.jpg
s3://videos/2026/video2.jpg
s3://videos/2026/videoN.jpg
s3://videos/2027/video1.jpg
s3://videos/2027/video2.jpg
s3://videos/2027/videoN.jpg
.fi
.SH CREATING ARCHIVES
The
.B \-\-create
operation always needs an S3 URI. To select everything, pass
.B s3://
explicitly; omitting the operand is an error.
.PP
Back up everything in S3:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-c \-f media.tar s3://
.fi
.PP
The same backup, with both buckets named explicitly:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-c \-f media.tar s3://photos s3://videos
.fi
.PP
Back up one bucket:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-c \-f photos.tar s3://photos
.fi
.PP
Back up one prefix:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-c \-f photos-2026.tar s3://photos/2026
.fi
.PP
Back up two prefixes:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-c \-f media-2026.tar s3://photos/2026 s3://videos/2026
.fi
.PP
Without
.BR \-f ,
the archive is streamed to standard output:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-c s3://photos/2026 >photos-2026.tar
.fi
.PP
Each bucket becomes a
.B BUCKET/
directory entry. Its objects are stored as regular
.B BUCKET/KEY
entries and streamed directly from S3 into libarchive. The bucket entry comes
first, so an empty bucket still appears in the archive.
.PP
Archive creation is not atomic. An object may change between the S3 LIST and
GET requests.
.PP
With
.BR \-v ,
archived entry names are written to standard error without the
.B s3://
prefix. A tar stream written to standard output remains untouched.
.SS Archive format
S3-specific information is stored in namespaced SCHILY extended attributes:
.PP
.nf
SCHILY.xattr.user.s3ar.format=1
SCHILY.xattr.user.s3ar.bucket-acl=public-read,custom
SCHILY.xattr.user.s3ar.metadata.NAME=VALUE
.fi
.PP
Every bucket and object carries the format marker. This prevents metadata from
older archives whose name begins with
.BR s3ar.
from being mistaken for part of the current format. On a filesystem, the
attribute names are
.B user.s3ar.format
and
.B user.s3ar.bucket-acl
for bucket directories, plus
.B user.s3ar.metadata.NAME
for objects.
.PP
GNU tar can restore these attributes on filesystems that support them, but
xattr handling must be enabled explicitly:
.PP
.nf
mkdir restored
tar --xattrs --xattrs-include='user.s3ar.*' -xf backup.tar -C restored
getfattr -d -m 'user.s3ar.*' restored/BUCKET
getfattr -d -m 'user.s3ar.*' restored/BUCKET/KEY
.fi
.PP
This is useful for inspecting or testing S3 metadata. It does not make the
archive a general filesystem backup.
.PP
Unsafe object keys containing empty,
.BR . ,
or
.B ..
path components are rejected.
.SH EXTRACTING ARCHIVES
Without an S3 URI,
.B \-\-extract
restores the whole archive:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-x \-f media.tar
.fi
.PP
To restore only part of the archive, use the URI syntax described in
.BR "SELECTING S3 RESOURCES" .
Restore the
.B photos
bucket:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-x \-f media.tar s3://photos
.fi
.PP
Restore one prefix:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-x \-f media.tar s3://photos/2026
.fi
.PP
Restore two prefixes:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-x \-f media.tar s3://photos/2026 s3://videos/2026
.fi
.PP
Without
.BR \-f ,
the archive is read from standard input. Selection URIs work the same way as
with an archive file:
.PP
.nf
cat media.tar | s3ar \-x s3://photos/2026
.fi
.PP
Bucket entries create buckets that do not exist yet. Object data is streamed
from libarchive into S3 PUT requests, overwriting objects with the same keys.
.PP
.B s3ar
accepts only tar archives containing top-level bucket directories and regular
.B BUCKET/KEY
object entries.
It rejects absolute paths, empty,
.BR . ,
and
.B ..
components, links, and other entry types. It accepts uncompressed and
zstd-compressed archives. A URI that matches no archive entry is an error.
.PP
Each
.B BUCKET/
entry must appear exactly once and before its
.B BUCKET/KEY
objects.
.B s3ar
creates or checks the bucket before uploading anything into it. Selecting an
object or prefix also processes its bucket entry; unselected buckets are not
created.
.PP
On entries marked with
.BR SCHILY.xattr.user.s3ar.format=1 ,
.B SCHILY.xattr.user.s3ar.metadata.NAME
values are restored as S3 user metadata. On unmarked entries from older s3ar
releases,
.B SCHILY.xattr.user.NAME
is accepted instead, including legacy names that begin with the now-reserved
.B s3ar.
prefix. Unknown format-marker values are rejected.
.PP
Bucket ACL summaries are informational and are not restored. New buckets and
uploaded objects use private ACLs. With
.BR \-v ,
restored names are written to standard error without the
.B s3://
prefix.
.SH LISTING BUCKETS AND OBJECTS
.SS Listing buckets
.B \-\-list-buckets
lists all bucket names without listing their objects:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-\-list-buckets
.fi
.PP
Bucket names are written to standard output, one per line:
.PP
.nf
photos
videos
.fi
.PP
Add
.B \-v
to include the ACL summary after a space:
.PP
.nf
photos acl=public-read,custom
videos acl=private
.fi
.PP
An ACL unavailable from the endpoint is shown as
.BR acl=unavailable .
All positional arguments to
.B \-\-list-buckets
are ignored. A URI such as
.B s3://bucket/prefix
neither filters the output nor accesses that resource.
.SS Listing objects
.B \-\-list-objects
needs one or more S3 URIs. It prints matching objects as
.BR BUCKET/KEY ,
one per line and without the
.B s3://
prefix:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-\-list-objects s3://photos/2026
.fi
.PP
produces:
.PP
.nf
photos/2026/photo1.jpg
photos/2026/photo2.jpg
photos/2026/photoN.jpg
.fi
.PP
The URI
.B s3://
lists objects from every bucket. Buckets are processed in sorted order. Object
listings keep S3 ordering and continue across pages of up to 512 objects. Empty
buckets produce no output because bucket names are not printed separately.
.PP
A bucket URI lists the whole bucket. An object URI lists the exact object
first, if it exists, followed by matching descendants in S3 order.
.PP
Add
.B \-v
to include the byte size, modification time as a Unix timestamp, and ETag.
The fields are separated by spaces. Spaces in names are encoded as
.BR %20 :
.PP
.nf
photos/2026/photo1.jpg 1234 1788104297 "3472a7..."
.fi
.PP
An unavailable ETag is shown as
.BR \- .
The values come directly from the object listing;
.B s3ar
does not send a HEAD request for every object.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The listing or help completed successfully.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage, invalid configuration, allocation failure, S3 failure, or
output failure.
.PP
Fatal errors go to standard error and start with
.BR s3ar: .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tar (1)
